13:41:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your dad's experience wasn't wrong for what he was seeing—it's just that the underlying reason wasn't "it's permanent," it's that the licensing landscape for most of the 20th century didn't force studios to revisit deals very often. That created the *illusion* of permanence. **Why classic movies seemed unchanged** - **Fewer formats existed.** For most of the 20th century, a movie played in theaters, then maybe on network TV, then home video (VHS) came along later. There were only a few "buckets" of rights to negotiate, and studios often secured what they needed for the formats that existed at the time. - **Re-releases were rarer.** Films weren't constantly being repackaged for new platforms the way they are now. A movie made in 1955 might not see any new "release event" for decades, so there was no trigger to renegotiate music rights. - **TV syndication deals were often long-term and broad**, because in that era rights holders didn't anticipate a future of streaming, VOD, DVD box sets, etc. They sold what seemed like comprehensive rights *for the media that existed*, and it wasn't tested until new media came along. **Where it started breaking down** Streaming media is really what exposed the difference. Once services like Netflix, Disney+, and others needed digital streaming rights for older catalogs, studios discovered that many decades-old licenses never anticipated (or covered) that use case. That's when you started seeing: - TV shows from the 80s/90s missing their original music (like *WKRP*, *Married... with Children*) - Older films with swapped songs on streaming versions **So it's less that the rule changed, and more that new technology exposed a rule that was always there** The licenses were always time/format-limited in principle—it's just that for decades, no one hit the edges of those limits because the "next format" didn't come along fast enough to matter. Once film distribution exploded into dozens of formats and platforms, the temporary nature of these licenses became visible in a way it never had to be before. So your dad's memory is accurate as an *observation*—but the reason wasn't some permanent-ownership rule. It was that the licensing gaps just hadn't been tested yet in his era.
